// The Gettier problem, cross-examined by the engine.
//
// Smith has strong evidence that Jones will get the job, and has counted that Jones
// has ten coins. So Smith is JUSTIFIED in believing "the man who gets the job has
// ten coins." The belief turns out TRUE — but because *Smith* gets the job and Smith
// happens to also have ten coins. Justified, true, believed — yet it was luck, not
// the evidence, that made it true. The classic verdict: this is NOT knowledge.
//
// The vetted JTB library (epistemics.vrf) is imported; this file only supplies the
// case. The model writes facts, the library supplies the principles — and the
// engine derives, mechanically, that the case falls short of knowledge.
//
// Expected result: CONSISTENT. `NOT is_knowledge` is DERIVED (from the imported
// gettier_defeats_knowledge premise), PROVED, and the local HENCE step holds.

DOMAIN gettier

IMPORT "epistemics.vrf"

// Smith's evidence, and the lucky fact that he — not Jones — got the job.
FACT smith counted_the_coins
FACT smith got_the_job

// The three JTB credentials, written as facts about the imported claim atom.
// The claim is true BECAUSE Smith got the job (the lucky truth-maker, NOT the
// evidence) — BECAUSE names that ground and the engine checks it actually holds.
FACT epistemics.claim is_true BECAUSE smith got_the_job
FACT epistemics.claim is_believed

// ...but the evidence pointed at Jones, so luck bridged the gap: a Gettier case.
FACT epistemics.claim is_gettiered

// Justification is not asserted raw — it is DERIVED from the evidence by a local
// principle, so we can show the checked derivation step below.
RULE evidence_justifies:
    WHEN smith counted_the_coins
    THEN epistemics.claim is_justified

// The epistemic voices. Belief is non-factive (reported, never a conflict);
// knowledge is factive (knowing an established-FALSE atom would be a CONFLICT).
BELIEVES smith epistemics.claim is_true         // Smith holds the belief — and it's true
KNOWS    smith smith counted_the_coins          // and he knows his own evidence

// The claim cannot be both true and false at once.
PREMISE not_both_ways:
    EXCLUSIVE
        epistemics.claim is_true
        epistemics.claim is_false

// Proof kernel — ask the theory directly: does it entail this is NOT knowledge?
PROVE NOT epistemics.claim is_knowledge

// ...and show the local one-step derivation of the justification condition,
// checked ref by ref (the RULE plus the written evidence fact).
HENCE epistemics.claim is_justified FROM evidence_justifies, smith counted_the_coins

// Targeted abduction: does the coin-counting explain the justification? (yes —
// the theory plus that hypothesis entails is_justified.)
TRY smith counted_the_coins FOR epistemics.claim is_justified

CHECK BIDIRECTIONAL
